@Michael: Well, I wouldn't call myself an expert on the Big Bang, but I can certainly speak to the physics of compressing matter. If the speed of light were slows down, then all electromagnetic forces would also be slower to propagate. That means the electrons in atoms would take more time to respond to the pull of nuclei, and their orbitals would move closer in. This could result in much more compact matter overall, with atoms taking up a smaller physical space. –  Colin K May 5 '11 at 14:06
@Colin: If light speed were reduced, would "everything" compress, or would only things at large scales (e.g. planets, galaxies) compress? Would subatomic particles still be unaffected, or would a proton, for example, be smaller? –  oosterwal Aug 4 '11 at 17:15
I think the reason light speed is considered a limit is because anything with mass cannot be accelerated to the speed of light because of increasing relativistic mass. If the speed of light was half what it is, then most things should be moving at approximately $\sqrt{1/2}$c instead of approximately $c$, so I don't see why physical lengthscales should change. –  Peter Shor Aug 7 '11 at 20:15
